# Git & PR Workflow Rules

Invoke this explicitly (`@git-workflow`) when committing or preparing a PR
description — it's intentionally not auto-loaded on every file edit.

## End-of-task ship loop

When implementation is done and local gates pass, invoke **`ship-pr`**
(`.cursor/skills/ship-pr/SKILL.md`) or say *ship it* / *land this*. That skill
runs: branch → scoped commit → push → PR → wait for CI → merge when green →
`main` synced with `origin`. Do not merge with failing checks.

## Commit messages

- Conventional Commits style: `feat:`, `fix:`, `refactor:`, `test:`,
  `docs:`, `chore:`. Scope when useful: `feat(domain-tax): add Section 7E threshold edge case`.
- Reference the ADR number in the commit body if the change implements or
  modifies a documented architectural decision.

## Before proposing a PR is ready

- `turbo run test lint typecheck` passes locally.
- Any touched `packages/domain/*` or `packages/verification/*` function
  still has up-to-date TSDoc and a passing test for the new behavior.
- Any new public page under `(marketplace)` has `generateMetadata` and a
  sitemap entry — see `seo.mdc`.
- Any new or changed dependency is reflected in
  `docs/architecture/dependency-baseline.md` — see `dependency-management.mdc`.
- A one-line summary of *why*, not just *what*, is in the PR description —
  "what" is visible in the diff; "why" is what a reviewer actually needs.

## Scope discipline

- One logical change per PR. A tax-rate fix and an unrelated UI tweak
  belong in separate PRs even if they're both small — this keeps the
  audit trail (and rollback story) clean for the parts of this codebase
  that touch money and compliance.
